"text": "Why do hotel beds feel comfy but you still can't fall asleep? You see, Brown University scientists watched people sleep in a lab, two nights in a row. On night one, something strange happened. The right half of the brain slept deeply. The left half stayed lighter, on guard. When odd beeps reached that guarding half, sleepers woke up more often and faster. It's a night watchman, listening for danger in a place it doesn't know yet. Dolphins and ducks do this every night. By your second night, the watchman clocks off. So it isn't the bed, it's your brain guarding you.",
